4. THE CONSUMER SWEEP -- PASS, with three findings (none a totals defect)

4a. The named regression, verified by mutation, not by reading.

The merged tree fixes internal/handlers/dashboard/handlers.go:1051 with an
explicit `if t.TransactionType == models.Transfer { continue }` ahead of the
`if Income {+=} else {-=}` branch, and ships
TestBuildCumulativeChartData_SkipsTransfers (handlers_test.go:328-366).

I did not take the test's existence as proof it holds anything. I copied the
tree to a scratch directory outside the repo (tar, excluding .git and data),
deleted ONLY the three-line skip, and re-ran:

    MUT1: remove the Transfer skip from buildCumulativeChartData
    --- FAIL: TestBuildCumulativeChartData_SkipsTransfers (0.00s)
        handlers_test.go:362: cumulative[1] = 1000, want 5000
                              -- a transfer day moves the line by nothing
        handlers_test.go:365: cumulative[2] = -2500, want 4500
                              -- only the 500 grocery run is spending
    FAIL	budget2/internal/handlers/dashboard	0.003s

That is the adjudication's claim reproduced numerically on a $2,000 paired
transfer: the line reads 1000 instead of 5000, a $4,000 error, both legs
subtracted. The test genuinely holds the fix.

I ran the same mutation discipline on the other two load-bearing guards:

    MUT2: remove `if TransactionType == models.Transfer { continue }` from
          classifier.ClassifyTransactions
    --- FAIL: TestClassifyTransactions_SkipsTransferRows
          "SCHWAB MONEYLINK TRANSFER": type = "Outflow", want "Transfer"
          "TRANSFER IN FROM SCHWAB":   type = "Income",  want "Transfer"
    --- FAIL: TestLoadData_TransfersAreClassifiedNotDropped
    --- FAIL: TestLoadData_MetricsExcludeEveryTransferRow
    --- FAIL: TestResolveTransfer_ConfirmSurvivesReload
    FAIL	budget2/internal/services/classifier
    FAIL	budget2/internal/services/dataloader

The relabel-but-consumers-unchanged failure of ruling 2026-08-16f is caught at
four sites, including the end-to-end metrics assertion. The fixture is
deliberately hostile: the credit leg is described "TRANSFER IN FROM SCHWAB",
which hits classifier.IncomeKeywords, so a leak surfaces in Total Income rather
than quietly cancelling against the debit leg.

4b. My own enumeration of consumers. I did not use the adjudication's list.

Method: `grep -rn "models.Income|models.Outflow|models.Transfer"` over all
non-test Go; `grep` for every `FilterByType`, `SumAmount`, `SumAbsAmount`,
`MonthlyTotals`, `CategoryTotals`, `GroupByMonth/Category/Date`, every
`range .*\.Transactions`, and every `+=`/`-=` over an Amount, across handlers,
services, cmd, mcpsvc AND the html templates. Then I read each hit's
surrounding function to see what set it was standing on.

Sites that filter by type and are therefore correct for free (each verified by
reading the enclosing function, not by the call site alone):

  metrics.Calculate                      metrics.go:100-101 (income/outflows;
    every later number in that function -- totals, savings rate, healthcare,
    living, budget variance, all five trend arrays -- is derived from those two
    sets and healthcareOutflows, so nothing there can see a Transfer row)
  dashboard  handlers.go:314-315, 472-473, 593, 766, 898, 978
  explorer   handlers.go:173/175, 185, 189, 312/314, 324, 328
  majorexpenses handlers.go:346  (and engine.go:77 iterates unfiltered but
    every caller -- dashboard:778, majorexpenses:348, curate/register.go:204 --
    hands it an Outflow-filtered set; engine.go:282,323 additionally guard
    `!= models.Outflow`)
  insights   trends.go:35, 119-120, 185, 273-274, 303; recurring.go:223
  mcpsvc     spend/summary.go:80, 113, 135; spend/search.go:148/150;
             curate/register.go:197
  anomalies.go:161, pricecreep.go:81, spend/insights.go:145
    -- all three gate on `== models.Outflow && Amount < 0`, the safe direction
  near_duplicates.go:58 -- `!= models.Outflow` -> skip; transfers drop out of
    near-dup detection automatically, which is what design §2 predicted

Sites that do NOT filter by type. There are five. I found two the adjudication
does not name:

  (i)  dashboard/handlers.go:1044 buildCumulativeChartData -- THE named one.
       Fixed, mutation-proven above.

  (ii) internal/services/dataloader/major_expense_names.go:49 -- NOT in the
       adjudication's list. It reads `if t.TransactionType == models.Income {
       continue }`, i.e. it stamps MajorExpenseName on everything that is not
       Income. This is literally the "not Income means expense" shape, and
       transfer classification runs before it (loader.go:354-356 then :377), so
       Transfer rows now reach it where the old drop-on-load filter meant they
       never did. Assessed: NOT a defect. MajorExpenseName is a label, not a
       total; I traced every consumer of it -- dashboard:983 merchantTotals,
       insights/trends.go:39,44, majorexpenses handlers/engine -- and all of
       them stand on an Outflow-filtered set, so a stamped Transfer row cannot
       enter any total. The visible effect is that a user-declared
       IsInternalTransfer major expense now labels its own rows in the ledger,
       which is desirable. Reported because item 4 asks for it.

  (iii) web/templates/pages/explorer.html:711, 712, 715 -- NOT in the
       adjudication's list, and the only user-visible consequence I found.
       `{{if eq .TransactionType "Income"}}` green/"+" `{{else}}` red /
       "Expense" badge. A Transfer row therefore renders red and badged
       "Expense". Assessed: NOT an A3 failure. The brief's Out of scope is
       explicit ("No UI (the transfers page is A7, the dashboard card A8)"),
       my own item 8 requires that no UI work leaked in, and the explorer's
       own totals on that page (handlers.go:185/189, 324/328) are type-filtered
       and correct. FLAGGED FOR A7: the ledger needs a Transfer badge, or the
       page will call every classified transfer an expense.

  (iv) mcpsvc/spend/search.go:157 `sum := ts.SumAmount()` when the caller
       passes no `type`. Transfers are now inside the searched set, so they are
       inside that sum. Assessed: NOT a defect. It is the signed sum of the
       rows being returned, not an income or expense total; a paired
       transfer's two legs are equal and opposite and cancel to exactly zero,
       and an external leg contributing its own signed amount is honest for a
       "sum of what you are looking at" figure.

  (v)  internal/services/accounts/balance.go:64 and :137 sum tx.Amount for an
       account with no type filter at all. Assessed: CORRECT, and improved by
       A3. A transfer really does move money, so a balance MUST include it.
       Under the old drop-on-load filter every pattern-matching transfer was
       deleted before balances were computed, so BalanceAt and the anchor-drift
       check were silently wrong by the size of every transfer. A3 fixes that
       as a side effect. This is the one place where "not filtering by type" is
       the right answer, which is why a blanket "add a Transfer skip
       everywhere" rule would have been wrong.

Nothing else in the repo does arithmetic over transaction amounts. mcpsvc/plan
and internal/services/retirement deal in models.IncomeSource (projection
inputs), not transactions; cmd/validate only asserts on HTML strings.

Regression cover exists at both real consumer sites, which is what the brief's
requirement 6 asks for: TestCalculateMetrics_ExcludesTransfers
(metrics_test.go:349-397, asserts TransactionCount 5 with TotalIncome 5000 /
TotalExpenses 1500 / NetSavings 3500 / SavingsRate / IncomeTrend / ExpensesTrend
against 9000+9000+7000 of transfers) and
TestBuildCumulativeChartData_SkipsTransfers.


5. AUTO-PAIR DISCIPLINE -- PASS, each rule mutation-proven

Reading transfers.go: the auto-pair pass (lines 183-206) passes candidates() an
`extra` gate of `!ambiguous[j] && (pattern[i] || pattern[j])`, so a pattern-less
match can never reach pairLegs; isCandidatePair (366-375) returns false on
`a.AccountID == b.AccountID`; a tie from nearest() (379-393) sets ambiguous and
routes to addSuspected instead of guessing. Reading is not proof, so:

    MUT3: `return !ambiguous[j] && (pattern[i] || pattern[j])`
       -> `return !ambiguous[j]`   (pattern gate removed from auto-pair)
    --- FAIL: TestClassify_PatternlessCoincidenceIsSuggestedNotPaired
    --- FAIL: TestLoadData_PatternlessCoincidenceIsSuggestedNotPaired
    --- FAIL: TestResolveTransfer_ConfirmSurvivesReload
    --- FAIL: TestResolveTransfer_RejectIsNeverSuggestedAgain
    --- FAIL: TestResolveTransfer_RejectsBadInput

    MUT4: delete `if a.AccountID == b.AccountID { return false }`
    --- FAIL: TestClassify_SameAccountEqualAmountDoesNotPair
    --- FAIL: TestLoadData_SameAccountEqualAmountDoesNotPair

    MUT5: `if len(best) == 1` -> `if len(best) >= 1` (tie silently picks first)
    --- FAIL: TestClassify_AmbiguousTieGoesToReview

All three rules are held by tests that fail when the rule is removed. The
coincidence fixture is the right one: a -$60 TARGET STORE debit and a +$60
ZELLE FROM PAT credit three days apart in different accounts, so auto-pairing
it would erase exactly one real expense and one real deposit -- the data-
integrity failure my brief singles out. Both the unit test
(transfers_test.go:110-142) and the end-to-end loader test
(transfers_test.go:204-234) assert neither row is typed Transfer AND that the
pair lands in the review queue with Reason == ReasonAmountMatch.

FLAG (advisory, not a failure): the "contagious ambiguity" insight the
adjudication singles out is NOT actually covered.

    MUT6: delete `ambiguous[j] = true` from the tie branch (transfers.go:203)
    ok  	budget2/internal/services/transfers
    ok  	budget2/internal/services/dataloader

The whole suite still passes. The reasoning in the code comment is sound and I
believe the guard is correct -- withdrawing a tied row as a *candidate* as well
as a subject needs a 4-row fixture (two independent ties sharing a row) that no
test builds. The adjudication's claim "Its own tie test caught that" is
overstated: TestClassify_AmbiguousTieGoesToReview passes with the line removed,
because `ambiguous[i]` alone is enough to defuse that particular 3-row fixture.
Behavior is right; the coverage claim is not. Worth a follow-up test, not a
rejection.


6. DECISIONS PERSIST -- PASS

transfer_decisions.go stores a {pair_key, stable_ids[2], verdict, decided_at}
map under transferDecisionsFile, read through the same beginWrite/SharedTx
sequence as the other sidecars; a missing or empty file returns an empty map
rather than an error. ResolveTransfer (transfers.go:95-124) rejects an empty
key, an unknown verdict, and a key that names no pair from the last load's
queue -- so it cannot write a silent no-op entry. applyDecisions
(transfers.go:288-332) runs BEFORE the auto-pair pass, iterates keys in sorted
order for determinism, pairs confirms regardless of pattern, and returns the
rejected set which candidates() then excludes everywhere -- which is exactly
what "never suggested again" has to mean.

Proven end to end across a real reload, not just in the unit layer:
TestResolveTransfer_ConfirmSurvivesReload (transfers_test.go:236-287) confirms
the pattern-less TARGET/ZELLE pair, calls loader.LoadData() again, and asserts
both rows come back Transfer/paired sharing the recorded pairKey, that the
queue is now empty, that FilteredTransfers() rose 3 -> 5, AND that
metrics.Calculate still reports TotalIncome 12.50 / TotalExpenses 84.12 -- the
newly confirmed pair leaves the totals too.
TestResolveTransfer_RejectIsNeverSuggestedAgain (289-316) reloads after a
reject and asserts the queue is empty, neither row is typed Transfer, and the
unrelated real transfer is untouched at 3.
TestClassify_RejectedPairIsNotAutoPairedEither (281-303) covers the harder
case: a rejected pair that DOES match a pattern still must not auto-pair.


