8. NO SCOPE LEAK -- PASS

Dedup: deduplicateTransactions' body is untouched -- the git diff shows only
context lines for it. StableID: internal/services/dataloader/stable_id.go and
internal/models StableIDFor do not appear in git status at all. Near-duplicate
engine: near_duplicates.go untouched. No web/, no internal/services/mcpsvc/,
no retirement or projection file is modified -- confirmed by the manifest and
by git status. So nothing from A5/A7/A8/A9 leaked in.

One pipeline change deserves stating rather than passing over. The dedup call
moved from AFTER classifier.ClassifyTransactions to BEFORE it
(loader.go:354-356). That is mandated, not incidental: design §2's "New
pipeline order" block and GLOSSARY "Load pipeline order" steps 5-7 both put
exact dedup ahead of transfer classification, with the stated reason that a
duplicated row must not manufacture a phantom pair candidate. The consequence
is that ComputeHash (date|lowercased-description|%.2f amount,
models/transaction.go:96-104) now sees pre-normalization amounts, so the narrow
class of rows that only collided AFTER sign normalization -- e.g. a -1500 and a
+1500 row sharing a date and an income-keyword description, which the old order
collapsed into one -- now survive as two rows. I judge that the more correct
behavior, and the full suite including the dataloader dedup coverage tests
passes, but it is a real behavioral consequence of a required reorder and
should be on the record rather than discovered later.


RULING 2026-08-16e (count assertion before loop assertion)

I checked every loop that carries an assertion in all five new/modified test
files. transfers_test.go (services): lines 61, 100, 137, 161, 173, 252, 398 --
all preceded by a count assertion (res.Paired / res.External /
len(res.Suspected) / len(res.Transactions)). transfers_test.go (dataloader):
lines 92, 187, 213, 259 preceded by ts.Len(); 155-156 preceded by
TransactionCount == 5 and followed by Transfer-set Len == 3; 308 preceded by a
queue-length assertion and findByDescription itself fatals unless exactly one
row matches. classifier_test.go's new loop is preceded by len(result) != 3.

ONE FLAG: internal/services/dataloader/transfers_test.go:343, in
TestLoadData_TransferIsNotANearDuplicateCandidate --

    for _, txn := range ts.Transactions {
        if txn.TransactionType == models.Transfer && txn.DuplicatePairKey != "" {

has no preceding count assertion of any kind, so it passes vacuously on an
empty or transfer-free set. It is a documentation test for a property already
guaranteed structurally by near_duplicates.go:58, and every other test in the
file pins the same fixture at 5 rows, so the risk is nil -- but it is exactly
the shape the ruling names and I am flagging it as required.


REQUIRED TEST LIST -- all nine present

clean cross-account pair auto-pairs (TestClassify_AutoPairsCleanCrossAccountPair
+ TestLoadData_TransfersAreClassifiedNotDropped); metrics exclude both legs
(TestLoadData_MetricsExcludeEveryTransferRow +
TestCalculateMetrics_ExcludesTransfers); classified rows stay in the ledger
(same two); pattern-less coincidence suggested not paired (unit + loader);
ambiguous tie to review (TestClassify_AmbiguousTieGoesToReview); external leg
with no pair key (TestClassify_ExternalLegHasNoPairKey); confirm survives
reload; reject never re-suggested; same-account pair does not pair (unit +
loader). Plus date-window boundary, closest-date-wins, same-sign rows, empty
input, pair-key order independence and input non-mutation.


CONCLUSION

PASS. Requirements 1-8 hold. The one bug the oracle could not see is fixed and
is held by a test that I proved fails without the fix; the pattern gate, the
different-accounts rule and the tie-to-review rule are each held by a test that
I proved fails without them; and my own independent enumeration of every
income/expense consumer in the repo -- including two sites the adjudication
never named and one, account balances, where not filtering by type is the
correct answer -- turns up no site where a Transfer row corrupts a number the
user reads. Three items are recorded for follow-up rather than as failures: the
explorer ledger badging every Transfer as "Expense" (A7 UI work, explicitly out
of A3's scope), the uncovered contagious-ambiguity guard, and the one loop
assertion missing a count assertion.

Repository state on exit: `git status --porcelain` is identical to the state I
found, plus this verdict file. I wrote nothing else in the repo; the mutation
work was done on a copy under the session scratchpad.
